item.text:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q026_protein_kinase_like_function_transfer","answer":"In stable SCOPe 2.08, the three catalytic-domain groups are distinct families within the same Protein kinase-like superfamily d.144.1 (SUNID 56112) and fold d.144: canonical protein-kinase catalytic subunits are d.144.1.7 (SUNID 88854), aminoglycoside/APH phosphotransferases are d.144.1.6 (SUNID 64411), and phosphoinositide 3-kinase catalytic domains are d.144.1.4 (SUNID 56171). Their shared superfamily supports probable common ancestry of the classified catalytic cores—stronger than mere fold similarity—and a shared kinase-like structural framework compatible with nucleotide-dependent phosphoryl transfer. It does not make them members of one close family, establish identical catalytic details, or extend homology to every region of the full-length proteins.

Substrate specificity and regulation are unsafe to transfer across these families. In particular, protein/peptide recognition and activation-loop-style control from canonical protein kinases, aminoglycoside-antibiotic specificity from APHs, and phosphoinositide head-group recognition, membrane targeting, and class-specific partner regulation from PI3Ks cannot be inferred from d.144.1 membership. Nor does the classification establish shared inhibitor sensitivity, nucleotide preference, regulatory partners, localization, oligomeric state, or pathway role.

A stronger transfer requires target-specific evidence: sequence alignment showing conservation of catalytic and specificity residues in equivalent structural positions; structural superposition and preferably substrate-, nucleotide-, and cofactor-bound structures defining the relevant pockets; biochemical kinetics and binding assays with the proposed donor and acceptor substrates; mutational tests of catalytic and recognition residues; and, for regulation, full-length domain-context, conformational, post-translational-modification, membrane-association, interaction-partner, cellular, and phylogenetic evidence. SCOPe hierarchy alone supplies none of those substrate- or regulation-specific demonstrations.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56112/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56171","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56171/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/64411","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/64411/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854/parents"]}]}
